            "findings": [],
            "evidence": [
              "Reviewed all 5 changed paths in full. The change exports Binding.Check (renamed from check) and Parse calls it with unchanged behavior, so there is no regression on the reply-parsing path.",
              "CheckExemption now validates the stored binding first, so a malformed base such as an option-like string is refused with ErrInvalidBinding before git sees it. This matches the stated ordering and the doc comment.",
              "engine/review/exempt_check_test.go covers option-like base, ref base, abbreviated head, bad digest and duplicate paths. Each case uses a risk and report that would also be refused, and the test asserts that neither ErrHighRisk nor ErrNotClear is returned. That proves the ordering at the observable level.",
              "engine/reviewresult/binding_check_test.go covers the accepting cases (a valid binding, and an empty path list) and the refusing cases, asserting both that ErrInvalidBinding is wrapped and that the offending member is named.",
              "Valid stored exemptions with well-formed bindings behave as before, so there is no baseline regression. Existing exemption tests that use well-formed bindings are unaffected by the added check."
            ]
          }
